WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.620
Alerts are signals that require review.

00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:06.000
They are not automatically incidents, and they are not automatically accurate.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.495
A good alert gives enough context for a reviewer to understand what happened,

00:00:09.495 --> 00:00:13.730
why it may matter, which system or identity is involved, how confident the detection is,

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:16.000
and what next step should be considered.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.975
Alerting is useful only when it fits into a triage workflow.

00:00:18.975 --> 00:00:23.000
Triage evaluates severity, confidence, context, ownership, and escalation need.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.000
Severity considers potential impact.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.195
Confidence considers how likely the signal is to represent a real problem.

00:00:28.195 --> 00:00:33.060
Context considers asset criticality, user behavior, recent changes, known vulnerabilities,

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:33.940
and related alerts.

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.975
Ownership determines who can investigate or remediate.

00:00:36.975 --> 00:00:41.000
Escalation determines whether the issue should become an incident, a ticket, a risk item,

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.940
or a tuning opportunity.

00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:45.980
False positives and false negatives are both important.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.330
A false positive is an alert or finding that reports a problem that did not occur as

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:50.000
described.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.000
Too many false positives create alert fatigue,

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.940
where reviewers become overwhelmed and meaningful signals are easier to miss.

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:00.000
A false negative is a relevant problem or event that monitoring fails to detect.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.000
False negatives can leave serious risk invisible.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.245
Tuning should address both.

00:01:04.245 --> 00:01:06.980
Detection improves over time when teams document decisions.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.000
If an alert is repeatedly low value, tune it.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.980
If an alert finds real risk, improve context and response steps.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:16.000
If an incident reveals missing telemetry, add or repair data sources.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.255
If ownership is unclear, update routing.

00:01:18.255 --> 00:01:22.000
Continuous monitoring is strongest when triage produces feedback,

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.560
and feedback improves detection quality, response speed, and confidence in the program.
